{
  "description": "Deterministic resolution vectors for price_adjustments on binding fixed-price pricing options. One running total: starting at fixed_price, each firing row applies to it in declared order (a rate row adds or subtracts round half away from zero of running x rate, an amount row adds or subtracts its amount), so a 10% fee then a 10% fee is x1.21. Dutch index cards are expressed as fee or discount rates (factor 1.25 is a fee rate of 0.25, factor 0.80 a discount rate of 0.20). Selection rows fire at most once per dimension (the first valid matching row in declared order); always rows fire independently; valid_from/valid_until bound a row to a half-open window evaluated at the purchase start_time, and a flight that crosses a window boundary of a row that would otherwise apply is rejected. Price precision is the greatest of the currency minor-unit exponent, 4 for cpm/vcpm/cpc/cpcv/cpv/cpp, and the fewest decimals that exactly represent fixed_price and each amount, by numeric value; the resolved price is the exact running total and never below zero. Arithmetic MUST be exact decimal or integer. Each fired row is recorded in price_breakdown in declared order: a rate below 1 as its rate, otherwise (a fee rate of 1 or more, or an amount row) as its currency amount. A table is bound to its quote: it applies only to the account it was returned for and until the quote expires. A changed table is a new pricing_option_id, so the accepted pricing_option_id identifies exactly one immutable table, which both parties retain from discovery; a change the agreement already permits is priced from that table, never a later one, and the table grants no change right.",
